 record lows","change":"Rochester's nights got warmer. Its days didn't.","rain":"Rochester has gotten wetter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 38\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>38\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 43\u00b0F, and the record is 71\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>22\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Rochester has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Rochester's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Rochester has set <strong class=\"sig\">21 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 86 that chance alone would give, and <strong>95 record highs</strong>, close to the 86 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (75). Rochester's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 14, 1936.","t_chg":"Rochester's nights got warmer. Its days didn't.","p_chg":"Across 107 complete years: Rochester's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.33\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +0.44), about +4.6\u00b0F since 1887. Rochester's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.16 to +0.07) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Rochester has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Rochester's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.74 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.36 to +1.12), about +10.2 inches since 1887. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 34.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (55.16 in); the driest, 1910 (11.86 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 8 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}